  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced partnerships and communication for policy coherence to achieve decent work results</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Weak labour productivity has been a persistent challenge. In the last twenty years labour productivity growth has been constantly below the global average and has been declining since 2010 as shown in the &#x201c;Productivity growth, diversification and structural change in the Arab States&#x201d; published by the ILO in March 2022. Moreover, SMEs in the formal private sector in the Arab region have the lowest Total Factor Productivity (TFP) and hire less skilled labour. Building on the above reports and on the research on pro-productivity organizations, further efforts are needed to fill the knowledge gaps and engage in informed discussions that can put productivity growth and economic diversification for decent work at the heart of the policy agenda. including through supporting the development of national/regional productivity platforms or networks and the implementation of advocacy strategies. 
The Arab States region is also characterised by comparatively low inter-regional trade and participation in many of the major global supply chains. However, national development strategies in GCC countries prioritise economic diversification, including strategies to seek inter-regional cooperation, integrate into regional and global supply chains, attract and retain foreign investment. These dynamics create the potential for new entry points to engage constituents on the relevance of labour provisions in trade and investment agreements and responsible business conduct. The outcome will build the knowledge gaps, and the capacity of ILO tripartite constituents in these two areas of work and strengthen the ILO&#x2019;s leadership position on issues related to productivity and decent work and decent work in supply chains as well as the labour dimensions of trade and investment. This requires further knowledge, research and data on regional relevance of these instruments and closer engagement with EMBOs, UNGC, other international organisations and initiatives in the region.</narrative>
  </description>
